WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_devf_341'), 'After the rollback, the 5xx rate is back at 0.3% on v3.1.4 at 14:16. The fix, v3.2.1, adds GET /ready, which returns 503 unless every required variable is set and SELECT 1 on the database answers within 500 ms; the readinessProbe now calls /ready. To test the readiness check on its own, the team deploys v3.2.1 to staging with its start-up config check switched off and the old Deployment that still sets only DB_URL:

14:40:02 deploy checkout v3.2.1 as canary, weight 10%
14:40:38 pod checkout-staging-5d1-q8r2t: GET /ready -> 503 {"ready": false, "problems": ["config DATABASE_URL is not set", "database: OperationalError"]}
14:45:02 gate: canary pods ready 0/2 -> HOLD, no traffic sent to v3.2.1

Statement: "This staging run shows that the new readiness check would stop the same mistake before any user sent traffic to it."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The same missing variable now makes /ready return 503 with the reason, the canary has 0 of 2 ready pods, and the gate holds without sending traffic. That is how you verify a fix to a health check: reproduce the original failure on purpose and watch the check catch it. A check that has never been seen failing is not yet a check you can trust.', 6, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_devf_341') AND order_index = 6)
    RETURNING id
)
